177
00:08:17.129 --> 00:08:20.433
Let's actually go over visually
what really is going on.

178
00:08:20.433 --> 00:08:24.203
So in PEFT, parameter efficient fine
tuning, one technique of doing

179
00:08:24.203 --> 00:08:26.906
it is a very popular one is called LoRA.

180
00:08:26.906 --> 00:08:28.474
that stands for Low Rank Adaptation.

181
00:08:28.474 --> 00:08:31.444
And essentially what's happening is
you can see here there's a weight matrix.

182
00:08:31.444 --> 00:08:35.081
Let's say this is like a feedforward
layer in, in the transformer model.

183
00:08:35.248 --> 00:08:37.783
So just you can think of it
as just like some weights of the model,

184
00:08:37.783 --> 00:08:41.020
instead of fine tuning it directly
and changing those weights directly, let's

185
00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:45.591
actually tune these set of LoRA weights
that are external to the model and smaller,

186
00:08:45.791 --> 00:08:49.428
and let's actually tune those instead
so that it's much

187
00:08:49.428 --> 00:08:50.429
more computationally efficient.

188
00:08:50.429 --> 00:08:52.999
We only have to do what's known as back
propagation, basically

189
00:08:52.999 --> 00:08:56.002
learn on the LoRA weights
and not touch the main weights at all.

190
00:08:56.168 --> 00:08:58.271
And then when we're inferencing,
when we're done training,

191
00:08:58.271 --> 00:08:59.872
when we're done fine tuning the model,

192
00:08:59.872 --> 00:09:03.276
we can actually fuze
those LoRA weights back into the model.

193
00:09:03.276 --> 00:09:06.145
So that it takes
the same amount of efficiency.

194
00:09:06.145 --> 00:09:08.080
It takes the same amount of latency,
speed,

195
00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:09.515
of running through the model itself.

196
00:09:09.515 --> 00:09:12.652
And that fuzing process is
I'll just say it's using math.

197
00:09:13.586 --> 00:09:17.123
It's using math, Low Rank Adaptation
and basically, finding low rank matrices.

198
00:09:17.189 --> 00:09:19.559
So that's,
that's not what that process looks like.

199
00:09:19.559 --> 00:09:23.162
And then in, the MoME model, it's
a very similar technique.

200
00:09:23.162 --> 00:09:25.665
The LoRA adopters are still there.

201
00:09:25.665 --> 00:09:29.235
But in addition to those LoRA adopters,
you actually have an array of

202
00:09:29.235 --> 00:09:31.971
what's known as memory experts
weights as well, that you're tuning,

203
00:09:31.971 --> 00:09:34.273
and that you're sampling,
at every single stage

204
00:09:34.273 --> 00:09:38.077
where you do have, those adapters,
and you're sampling a subset of them

205
00:09:38.077 --> 00:09:42.181
that contain this understanding of facts
that have been learned from your data.

206
00:09:42.348 --> 00:09:45.551
and you're fuzing
those into the adopters themselves.

207
00:09:45.952 --> 00:09:49.655
And this makes it so that you can actually
grow your model via the memory experts

208
00:09:49.922 --> 00:09:53.626
in a way where you can get essentially
the intelligence of a huge model

209
00:09:53.826 --> 00:09:56.195
with the cost
and latency of a smaller model.

210
00:09:56.195 --> 00:09:59.899
So, you know, a common way of putting this
is calling it sparsely activated, model.

